{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q018_tal_effector_repeat_lengths","answer":"d2kq5a_, d3v6pa_, and d4cj9a_ belong to the same TAL effector family because they contain homologous TAL-repeat architecture; family membership does not require equal sequence lengths. Their length differences reflect different extents of the repeat region and experimental construct boundaries. A short repeat fragment and a much longer repeat array can therefore receive the same classification.

Canonical TAL repeats are approximately 34 residues long and contain two α-helices connected by a loop. Tandem repeats assemble into an elongated superhelix that can wrap around DNA. Adding repeats increases the array’s length without changing its basic structural organization. In SCOPe, a.298.1.1 is the family, nested within superfamily a.298.1, fold a.298, and class a (all-α proteins). Shared family membership supports common ancestry more specifically than merely sharing an all-α fold.

This classification does not make the three domains functionally interchangeable. Repeat-variable diresidues, typically at positions 12–13, and their order contribute to DNA-base recognition. Neither equal target specificity nor equal DNA-binding capability follows from family membership, especially for a short fragment. A short structure can inform local repeat modeling, but cannot alone establish the geometry or specificity of a complete array. Those require the actual repeat sequences and boundaries, structural context, and DNA-binding evidence.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
